body.insert(0,{'text':'Management presents ongoing and well-documented challenges, not least the need to deal with others’ demands and monitor their output. But new research from Australia appears to have uncovered a hidden benefit in later life. Embracing the responsibilities that come with rising through the corporate ranks boosts brain power. For the first time, researchers have identified a clear link between managerial experience throughout a person’s working life, and the integrity and larger size of the hippocampus – the area of the brain responsible for learning and memory. Using MRI imagery on subjects between 75 and 92 years of age, scientists discovered that the rate of shrinkage of the hippocampus with age in those with challenging careers was much lower than in those workers who were engaged in less complex daily tasks.'})
body[3]['text']='These results align with research into ageing and employment by Peter McDonald, from the Australian National University. His study of older workers showed that for men, as age progresses beyond age 65, managers and professionals make up increasing proportions of those employed, while technicians and trades workers, clerical and administrative workers, and machinery operators and drivers make up decreasing proportions. This would seem to indicate that those at a higher level in their career, who are forced to use their brain to solve complex issues, are mentally active, as well as fully employed, for longer.'
body[4]['text']='These findings in the area of brain health are encouraging for Australian policymakers facing the challenge of workers getting older and escalating rising health costs. It is estimated that over the next 35 years, the number of people aged 65–84 will double, and the number of people aged over 85 will quadruple. This will have a huge impact on the worker/non-worker dependency ratio. Currently, there are five working aged people for each Australian aged 65 years and over, but in the next 35 years this is set to drop to 2.7.'
for i,p in enumerate(body):p['label']=chr(65+i);p['text']=p['text'].replace('sayS Michael','says Michael')
groups=[info(14,['an instance of a researcher using animals in their testing','a reference to the economic and political challenges caused by an ageing workforce','a mention of how brains were examined','examples of occupations with fewer older workers','evidence that engaging in activities with others can slow mental decline'],8,'paragraphs',3),h.features(19,['Male and female managers’ brains showed similar patterns of development.','It is possible that physical activity could improve brain function.','There is a connection between preventing mental deterioration and managing large groups.','Brain function is reduced if the brain is underutilised.'],['Michael Valenzuela','Peter McDonald','Henriette van Praag','Ryuta Kawashima'],3,'researcher','Researchers',True),completion(23,['Valenzuela says managers use their brains in complex ways as they think up solutions, remember details and show {{23}} with their staff.','In 35 years’ time, there will be four times as many people as now older than {{24}}.','Kawashima describes the brain as just another {{25}}.','Kawashima’s games involve word, number and {{26}} activities.'],4,'ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER')]
